&lt;p&gt;Washington: The White House has listed India among the countries with which President Donald Trump &quot;secured new and improved trade agreements&quot;, even though the interim trade agreement the two sides announced in February has not yet been signed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The claim appears in a document titled &quot;President Trump's Second Term: Promises Made, Promises Kept, Results Delivered&quot;, posted on the White House website on Monday, the last day of campaigning before Tuesday's midterm elections. The document lists what it calls 127 promises Trump has kept.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&quot;By using tariffs as leverage, President Trump secured new and improved trade agreements with major U.S. trading partners covering more than half of global GDP - including the United Kingdom, European Union, Japan, China, India, Republic of Korea, Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand, Vietnam, the Philippines, and Cambodia,&quot; the document said.&lt;/p&gt;
